window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2017-05-02"]={"AD-006-17":"Resolution No. AD-006-17\nFrom: Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nSUMMER HOURS - 2017\nWHEREAS, the County has agreed to a ten-week period for a summer work schedule change and,\nWHEREAS, it was agreed that this new schedule will apply only to those deprutments and employees who are on a Monday through Friday, 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. schedule, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective June 25, 2017 through September 2, 2017 the work schedule of the County employees mentioned above will be 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. with the following regulations:\n1.  Any department required by local regulations or law to remain open until 5 :00 p.m. may be\nexempt from this schedule.\n2.  Each Deprutnient Head is authorized to return to the 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. schedule if the new\nhours cause operational problems, or if the employees abuse the new work schedule.\n3.  This agreement is for the ten-week period as stated above and will not be repeated without\nfmther Legislative action.\n4.  Public notice of the hours will be posted in all County offices affected.\nAny deviation from the above must be cleared with the Human Resources Department.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","CS-017-17":"Resolution No. CS-017-17\nFrom: Community Services & Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nABOLISH AND CREATE POSITION - SOCIAL SERVICES\nWHEREAS, a matter of practice the Depattment of Social Services reviews the need for the filling of vacant positions or to determine if they can be better utilized in other areas of the department, and\nWHEREAS, cun-ently the workload of the child welfare units have increased as a result of the number of children being referred for services due to increased child abuse reports, and\nWHEREAS, the housing unit has seen a substantial increase in the number of people who present themselves to the department as being homeless which has placed a significant burden on the existing staff, and\nWHEREAS, process reviews/revisions have been completed in other areas of the department, allowing us to make the determination that four ( 4) positions that are cmTently vacant, 1- Chief Social Services Worker, Job Group 12, 1- Account Clerical I, Job Group 4, 2-Social Services Workers, Job Group 7 are no longer necessary to fill, and\nWHEREAS, due to the increase in workload in the Child Welfare units, 2 (two) Caseworker positions, Job Group 9 would greatly help to ensure that children receive appropriate services, and\nWHEREAS, due to the increase in the number of people presented as homeless, an additional Employment Case Manager position, Job Group 9 is needed in the department's Housing unit to unsure that families are able to secure permanent housing and provide a stable environment for their children, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following positions be abolished effective May 8, 2017: Chief Social Services Worker, position #10208, Job Group 12 step 8 at $32.60 per hour, Social Services Worker position #1037 Job Group 7 step 4 at $20.97 per hour, Social Services Worker position #1184 Job Group 7 step 8 at $22.23 per hour, Account Clerical I position #1102 Job Group 4 step 8 at $18/84 per hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following positions be created and filled effective May 8, 2017: One (1) Employment Case Manager, Job Group 10 step 1 at $23.59 per hour, and two (2) Caseworker positions Job Group 9 step 1 each at $21.84 per hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following line item transfer be effectuated:\nFROM:\nA.22.6010.000 71010.10208  Chief Social Services Worker  $45,881\nA.22.6010.000 71010.1102  Account Clerical I  34,289\nTO:\nA.22.6010.000 71010.xxxx  Employment Case Manager  $28,072\nA.22.6010.000 71010.xxxx  Caseworker 1,2  26,049\nA.22.6010.000 71010.xxxx  Caseworker 1,2  26,049\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vittuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","CS-018-17":"Resolution No. CS-018-17\nFrom: Community Services & Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT IMPACT FUNDING\nNURSING DIVISION - DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH\nWHEREAS, the Nursing Division of the Niagara County Depaitment of Health provides a wide atTay of essential services, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Department of Health through the P2 Collaborative of Western New York, a New York not-for-profit corporation, has awarded the Nursing Division IMPACT (Initiatives for Multi-Sector Public Action) funds to support the implementation of the National Diabetes Prevention Program at a self-insured employer site and in primary care offices, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification, be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.20.4189.401 43489.06 Healthy Community Initiatives  $7,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.20.4189.401 71050.00 Overtime  $2,034.00\nA.20.4189.401 74250.01 Office Supplies  600.00\nA.20.4189.401 74250.03 Printing/Duplicating  800.00\nA.20.4189.401 74300.03 Mileage  700.00\nA.20.4189.401 74375.01 Advertising /Promotion  1,200.00\nA.20.4189.401 74600.02 Books/Subscriptions  300.00\nA.20.4189.401 74675.02 Central Printing  450.00\nA.20.4189.401 74675.03 Print Shop Supplies  100.00\nA.20.4189.401 74750.11 Med/lab/clinic supplies  250.00\nA.20.4189.401 78200.00 FICA  156.00\nA.20.4189.401 78100.00 Retirement  382.00\nA.20.4189.401 78300.00 Worker's Comp  28.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","CS-019-17":"Resolution No. CS-019-17\nFrom: Community Services & Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nACCEPT GRANT\nNIAGARA COUNTY HISTORIAN\nWHEREAS, an impo1tant part of the mission of the Niagara County Historian's Office is to serve as an advocate and resource in historic preservation of manuscripts and records that document a community's past and constitute a community's material culture, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Historian's Office has served as custodian of one-of-a-kind, never before microfilmed (or re-created in any fotmat) newspapers dating from 1826-1960, and\nWHEREAS, these important papers ai\u00b7e not held in any other repository and provide testimony of Niagai\u00b7a County's contributions to the Western New York region and the world, and\nWHEREAS, the collection is fragile and brittle (yet still legible), and is unlisted in the 1976 New York State Newspaper Project, and\nWHEREAS, since the collection is the only surviving copy, it has been determined that digitization of this rare collection is imperative, and\nWHEREAS, the Grigg-Lewis Foundation, Inc. has awarded the Niagara County Historian's office a grant in the amount of $51,000.00 for the purpose of preserving and protecting this fragile collection and the history it holds through digitization, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Historian will administer the grant, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the attached grant, subject to the approval of the County Attorney, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to accept the full amount of this grant: INCREASE REVENUE\nA.10.1410.000 42705.00  Gifts and Donations  $51,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA1410.000.74550.25  Records Maintenance  $51,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","CSS-024-17":"Resolution No. CSS-024-17\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE-BUDGET MODIFICATION\nEXPLOSIVE DETECTION CANINE TEAM GRANT PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a grant in the amount of $14,600 from the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services for the period April 1, 2017 through August 31, 2019 to protect Niagara County residents and infrastrnctme from terrorist attacks involving explosive devices, and\nWHEREAS, the grant will allow the Niagara County Sheriffs Office to purchase equipment and provide training to enhance explosive detection canine team capabilities, and\nWHEREAS, the 201 7 budget will need to be modified to allow for the spending of this grant, now therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chai1man of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the 2017 budget be modified:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000.44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $14,600\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000.72100.21  Law Enforcement Supplies  $11,900\nA.17 .3645 .000. 74 750.05  Law Enforcement Supplies  1,200\nA.17.3645.000.74600.03  Training and Education  1,500\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vi1tuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","CSS-025-17":"Resolution No. CSS-025-17\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nDISTRICT ATTORNEY BUDGET MODIFICATION\nUSE OF ASSETS FORFEITURE FUNDS -CRIME NIGHT OUT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office maintains its Local Asset Forfeiture Trust Account for the prosecution and reduction of targeted drug crimes, and\nWHEREAS, the use of locally forfeited funds is restricted by the New Yark State Department of Criminal Justice Services to the enhancement of the prosecution of related crimes and can be used to supplement but not supplant cunent resources, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office will allocate $500 to the Niagara Falls Block Club Council to fund their annual Crime Night Out, and\nWHEREAS, this is an allowable use under the Guidelines, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer's Office is hereby authorized to disburse $500 from the District Attorney's Local Assets Forfeiture Account A.02.1165.000.40036 into the District Attorney's operating budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications are effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.02.1165.000.40599.02  Appropriated Fund Balance Restricted Funds  $500\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.02.1165.000.74400.09  Payments to Other Agencies  $500\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","CSS-026-17":"Resolution No. CSS-026-17\nFrom: Community Services & Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nDISTRICT ATTORNEY- BUDGET MODIFICATION- REAPPROPRIATE GRANT FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office received funding from the Division of Criminal Justice Services in the amount of $15,000 for the period of October l, 2016 through September 30, 2017 to enhance law enforcement initiatives, and\nWHEREAS, funds were appropriated into the Dishfot Attorney's operating budget but not fully expended prior to year-end, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to re-appropriate those unspent into the 2017 operating budget to continue the initiative as directed by the Division of Criminal Justice Services, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.02.1165.000.43389.25  Division of Criminal Justice DA  $3,146.31\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS\nA.02.1165.000. 74400.10  Miscellaneous Expenses-Other  $3,146.31\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vi1tuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","CW-003-17":"Resolution No. CW-003-17\nFrom: Conunittee of the Whole\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION REQUESTING TO AMEND THE TAX LAW, IN RELATION TO\nCONTINUING TO AUTHORIZE NIAGARA COUNTY TO IMPOSE\nSALES AND COMPENSATING USE TAXES, PURSUANT TO\nSENATE BILL NO. S-5656 AND ASSEMBLY BILL NO. A-7465\nWHEREAS, in 2003 the County of Niagara requested the State of New York to amend Article 29, Sections, 1210, 1224 and 1262-n of the State of New York to authorize the County of Niagara to impose an additional one percent (1 %) rate of tax in addition to the three percent (3%) the County is authorized to impose and pursuant thereto the County imposed the additional one percent (1 %) rate and dedicated the funds so raised to the payment of the local share of Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 %) in the Niagara County sales tax which was imposed pursuant to Home Rule Message resolution FN-005-03 adopted January 21 , 2003 and pmsuant to imposition resolution FN-006-03 adopted January 22, 2003 to enable the County of Niagara to offset the cost of the local share of Medicaid, and \u00b7\nWHEREAS, the additional one percent (1%) was imposed for the period beginning March 1, 2003 and ending November 30, 2005, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara thereafter deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 %) in the Niagara County sales tax, as set forth in Home Rule Message resolution AD-017-05 adopted April 19, 2005 and imposition resolution AD-027-05 adopted July 12, 2005 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share of mandated Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the extension of the additional one percent (1 % ) rate was imposed for the period beginning December 1, 2005 and ending November 30, 2007, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara thereafter deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 %) in the Niagara County sales tax, as set forth in Home Rule Message resolution AD-017-07 adopted May 1, 2007 and imposition resolution CW-001-07 adopted August 7, 2007 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share mandated Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the extension of the additional one percent (1 %) rate was imposed for the period beginning December 1, 2007 and ending November 30, 2009, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara thereafter deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 % ) in the Niagara County sales tax, as set forth in Home Rule Message Resolution AD-012-09 adopted May 5, 2009 and imposition resolution CW-001-09 adopted July 28, 2009 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share mandated Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the extension of the one percent (1 %) rate was imposed from the period beginning December 1, 2009 and ending November 30, 2011, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara thereafter deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 % ) in the Niagara County sales tax, as set f01th in Home Rule Message Resolution AD-015-11 adopted May 17, 2011 and imposition resolution CW-001-11 adopted August 22, 2011 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share mandated Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the extension of the one (1 %) percent rate was imposed from the period beginning December 1, 2011 and ending November 30, 2013, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara thereafter deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 % ) sales tax, as set forth in Home Rule Message Resolution AD-009-13 adopted May 21, 2013 and imposition resolution AD-019-13 adopted August 6, 2013 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share of mandated Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the extension of the one (1%) percent rate was imposed from the period beginning December 1, 2013 and ending November 30, 2015, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara thereafter deemed it necessary to extend the additional one percent (1 %) sales tax, as set forth in Home Rule Message Resolution CW-009-15 adopted May 19, 2015 and imposition resolution CW-013-15 adopted September 9, 2015 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share of mandated Medicaid costs, and\nWHEREAS, the extension of the one percent (1 %) rate was imposed from the period beginning December 1, 2015 and ending November 30, 2017, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature hereby finds that it is necessary to fmther extend the additional one percent (1 %) sales tax for the period beginning December 1, 2017 and ending November 30, 2019 to enable the County of Niagara to continue to offset the cost of the local share of mandated Medicaid costs, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature requests the New York Legislature to authorize the County of Niagara to continue to impose an additional one percent ( 1 % ) rate of tax for the period beginning December 1, 2017 and ending November 30, 2019 in addition to the three percent (3%) the County is authorized to impose and to dedicate the funds from the additional one percent (1 %) rate to the payment of the local share of Medicaid costs, said authorization in the fmm of Senate Bill No. S-5656 and Assembly Bill No. A-7465, and\nbe it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Niagara County Legislature be authorized to execute the proper Home Rule Message documents and immediately forward them to the New York State Legislature. Chairman McNall implemented Rule 28 and turned the floor over to Legislator Nemi for discussion on CW-003-17\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Burmaster.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse and Wydysh","ED-008-17":"Resolution No. ED-008-17\nFrom: Economic Development & Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION OF THE NIAGARA COUNTY LEGISLATURE, AS THE ELECTED LEGISLATIVE\nBODY OF NIAGARA COUNTY, NEW YORK, IN ACCORDANCE WITH SECTION 147(f) OF THE\nINTERNAL REVENUE CODE OF 1986, AS AMENDED (THE \"CODE\"), APPROVING THE\nISSUANCE BY THE NIAGARA AREA DEVELOPMENT CORPORATION OF UP TO $17,000,000\nTAX-EXEMPT REVENUE BONDS (YOUNG MEN'S CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION BUFFALO\nNIAGARA PROJECT), SERIES 2017\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature (the \"Legislature\"), as the elected legislative body of Niagara County, New York (the \"County\") has been advised by the Niagara Area Development Corporation (the \"Issuer\") that, in order to assist in the financing of a certain Project (as defined below) for the benefit of Young Men's Christian Association Buffalo Niagara, a not-for-profit corporation organized and existing under and by virtue of the laws of the State of New York (for Young Men's Christian Association Buffalo Niagara Project itself, an affiliate or an entity on its behalf) (collectively, the \"Company\"), the Issuer proposes to issue, contingent upon the adoption of this Resolution by the Legislature, its Tax-Exempt Revenue Bonds (Young Men's Christian Association Buffalo Niagara Project), Series 2017, in an aggregate principal amount not to exceed $17,000,000 (the \"Bonds\"), and\nWHEREAS, proceeds of the Bonds will finance a certain project (the \"Project\") consisting of: (A) the financing of ce1tain improvements to be located at 1000 Locust Street Extension, Town of Lockport, New York (being more fully identified as tax map number 123.00-1-1) (the \"Land\"), consisting of (i) the construction of (a) an approximately 52,000 square-foot facility to provide traditional YMCA membership and program services such as fitness activities, youth spmis programs, summer day camp and preschool education and other services, and (ii) a related parking lot, all to serve the Town of Lockport and the surrounding communities (collectively, the \"Improvements\"), and (ii) the acquisition of and installation in and around the Improvements of certain machinery, equipment and other items of tangible personal property (the \"Equipment\", and together with the Land and the Improvements, the \"Facility\"); and (B) the paying of all or a pmtion of the costs incidental to the issuance of the Bonds, including issuance costs of the Bonds, capitalized interest and any reserve funds as may be necessary to secure the Bonds (the costs associated with items (A) and (B) above being hereinafter collectively refeITed to as the \"Project Costs\"), and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to Section 147(f) of the Code, interest on the Bonds will not be excluded from gross income for federal income tax purposes unless the issuance of the Bonds is approved by the Legislature after a public hearing to consider the issuance of the Bonds has been conducted following reasonable public notice, and\nWHEREAS, on May I, 2017 at 3:00 p.m., the Issuer held such a public hearing upon proper notice in compliance with the Code, and\nWHEREAS, to aid the Legislature in its deliberations, the Issuer has made available to the members of the Legislature prior to this meeting (a) the Company's application to the Issuer for financial assistance; (b) the notice of public hearing published by the Issuer in the Niagara Gazette, along with the affidavit of publication of such newspaper; and ( c) minutes of the Public Hearing, attended on behalf of the Issuer by Susan Langdon, Director of Project Development for the Issuer, and\nWHEREAS, the Legislature, after due consideration of the foregoing, as the \"applicable elected representative\" of the County, within the meaning of Section 147(f)(2)(E) of the Code, desires to approve the issuance of the Bonds, provided the principal, premium, if any, and interest on the Bonds shall be special obligations of the Issuer and shall never be a debt of the State of New York (the \"State\") or any political subdivision thereof, including without limitation, the County and neither the State nor any political subdivision thereof, including without limitation, the County shall be liable thereon.\nNOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED BY THE NIAGARA COUNTY LEGISLATURE:\nSection 1.  For the purpose of satisfying the approval requirement of Section 147(\u00b1) of the Code, on the\nrecommendation of the Economic Development Committee and the Administration Committee, the Legislature (as the elected legislative body of Niagara County, New York) hereby gives its approval of the issuance by the Issuer of its Bonds and related acts to be taken by the Issuer as part of the Project; provided, that the Bonds, and the premium (if any) and interest thereon, shall be special obligations of the Issuer and shall never be a debt of the State or any political subdivision thereof, including without limitation, the County, and neither the State nor\nany political subdivision thereof, including without limitation, the County, shall be liable thereon.  This\napproval is given pursuant to Section 147(\u00b1) of the Code for the sole purpose of qualifying the interest payable on the Bonds for exclusion from gross income for Federal income tax purposes pursuant to the provisions of Sections 103 and 141-150 of the Code.\nSection 2.  This Resolution shall be deemed to be made for the benefit of the holders of the Bonds.\nSection 3.  This Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nThe question of the adoption of the foregoing Resolution was duly put to a vote on roll call, which resulted as follows:\nYEA  NAY  ABSENT  ABSTAIN\nClyde L. Bmmaster  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nRebecca J. Wydysh  [  ]  [  ]  [ x ]  [  ]\nMark J. Grozio  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nOwen T. Steed  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nJason A. Zona  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nDennis F. V iituoso  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nKathryn L. Lance  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nRichard L. Andres  [  ]  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]\nRandy R. Bradt  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nDavid E. Godfrey  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nAnthony J. Nemi  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nWilliam J. Collins Sr.  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nWilliam Keith McN all  [  ]  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]\nJohn Syracuse  [  ]  [  ]  [ x ]  [  ]\nMichael A. Hill  [ x ]  [  ]  [  ]  [  ]\nThe Resolution was thereupon duly adopted.\nDated: May 2, 2017\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Collins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 2 Noes - McNall & Andres, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-071-17":"Resolution No. IF-071-17\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nGRISWOLD STREET BRIDGE OVER TRIBUTARY OF MUD CREEK\nFEDERAL AID LOCAL PROJECT AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Griswold Street Bridge over Tributary of Mud Creek project, town of Royalton, Niagara County, (the \"Project\"), is eligible for funding under Title 23 US Code, as amended, that calls for the app01tionment of the costs of such program to be borne at the ratio of 95% federal funds and 5% non-federal Funds, and\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of 100% of the non-federal share of the costs of the Preliminary Engineering Design Phases I-VI and Right-of-Way Incidentals phases of the project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal f01m, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara hereby authorizes the county of Niagara to pay in the first instance 100% of the federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the Preliminary Engineering Design Phases I-VI and Right-of-Way Incidentals phases of the Project or portions thereof, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nH651.15.5197.000 44597.01  Federal Aid Capital Const Hwy  $247,000\nH651.15.5197.000 43501.00  CHIPS  $ 13,000\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED APPROPRIATIONS:\nH65 l. l 5.5197 .000 72600.02  Infrastructure-Bridges  $260,000\nand be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $260,000 is hereby appropriated in account H651.15.5197.000 72600.02 and made available to cover the cost of participation in the above phases of the project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the amount required to pay the full federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the project's design phases exceeds the amount appropriated above, the county of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Depa1tment ofTransp01tation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chair of the Legislature of the county ofNiagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests for federal aid on behalf of the county of Niagara with the New York State Depaitment of Transportation, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the municipality's first instance funding of Project costs and permanent funding of the local share of federal aid eligible Project costs and all Project costs within appropriations therefor that are not so eligible, and be it fmiher\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transp011ation by attaching it to any necessary agreement in connection with the Project, and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-072-17":"Resolution No. IF-072-17\nFrom: Infrastmcture & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nW ALM ORE ROAD PAVEMENT PROJECT\nCONSULTANT CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Walmore Road Pavement project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H643.15.5112.000 72600.01 , Infrastmcture Roads, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, t_he County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Walmore Road Pavement Project be awarded to C&S Engineers, Inc., 499 Col Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for a contract amount not to exceed $185,000.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-073-17":"Resolution No. IF-073-17\nFrom: Infrastmcture & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nA WARD PUBLIC WORKS DIESEL FUEL ISLAND\nFACILITY CONSULTANT CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Diesel Fuel Island Facility project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H641.25.1620.000 72400.00, Land Improvements Expense, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Public Works Diesel Fuel Island Facility Project be awarded to Foit -Albert Associates, 763 Main Street, Buffalo, NY 14203, for a contract amount not to exceed $37,913.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chaitman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-074-17":"Resolution No. IF-074-17\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nA WARD GRISWOLD STREET BRIDGE OVER TRIBUTARY\nOF MUD CREEK CONSULT ANT CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Griswold Street Bridge over Tributary of Mud Creek project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H651.15.5197.000 72600.02, Infrastructure Bridges, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal fo1m, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Griswold Street Bridge over Tributary of Mud Creek Project be awarded to Watts Engineering & Architecture, PC, 95 Perry Street, Suite 300, Buffalo, NY 14203, for a contract amount not to exceed $218,700.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chaiiman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-075-17":"Resolution No. IF-075-17\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nNIAGARA COUNTY COURTHOUSE\nCONTRACT AMENDMENT\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-012-17, dated February 21, 2017, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Niagara County Court House Asbestos Abatement and Floor Preparation to Regional Environmental Demolition, Inc., for a contract amount of $32,200.00, and\nWHEREAS, it is recommended to increase the contract in the amount of $33,956.00, for additional square footage at the Co mt House to be remediated, for a revised contract amount of $66, 156.00, and\nWHEREAS, it is also recommended that a building at Bond Lake Park, be remediated, for a fee not to exceed, $3,200.00, for a revised contract price of $69,356.00, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal f01m, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract by $37,156 for the Niagara County Courthouse Project, for a revised contract amount of $69,356.00, to Regional Environmental Demolition Inc., 6281 Wendt Drive, Niagara Falls, NY 14304, be approved, and be it fuither\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-076-17":"Resolution No. IF-076-17\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND\nTHE LOCKPORT EXCHANGE CLUB\nWHEREAS, the Lockp01t Exchange Club have requested that the county of Niagara grant them permission to use a p01tion of the Davison Road Campus for the purpose of holding a Beef on Weck Sale, and\nWHEREAS, it is the wish of the Lockp01t Exchange Club to hold the Beef on Weck Sale on June 8, 2017, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Lockpo1t Exchange Club, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the license agreement between the County of Niagara and Lockp01t Exchange Club, the County Attorney will review said agreement for approval as to legal f01m, language and compliance, and now, therefore, it is\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chair of the County Legislature be, and hereby js, authorized to execute the license agreement between the county of Niagara and Lockp01t Exchange Club.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-077-17":"Resolution No. IF-077-17\nFrom: Infi:astructure & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND IABCA & INTERNATIONAL DOG SHOW\nWHEREAS, I AB CA & International Dog Show has requested that the county of Niagara grant them pe1mission to use Oppenheim Park, for the purpose of holding an International Dog Show Event, to be held May 19-21, 2017,and\nWHEREAS, the event will be successful in attracting many families from the area, and\nWHEREAS, it is the wish of the organizers to hold the event on May 19-21, 2017, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a f01mal agreement with IABCA & International Dog Show, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the license agreement between the county of Niagara and IABCA & Intemational Dog Show, the County Attorney will review said agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chai1man of the County Legislatw-e be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the license agreement between the county of Niagara and IABCA & International Dog Show.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-078-17":"Resolution No. IF-078-17\nFrom: Infrastrncture & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND\nTHE KAIHATSU FIELD HOCKEY CLINIC\nWHEREAS, the Kaihatsu Field Hockey Clinic has requested that the county of Niagara grant them rights to operate a field hockey clinic in an area situated in the County owned property on Lake Road, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the youth and other residents of both the town of Newfane in addition to Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, such program is operated on a not-for-profit basis, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the license agreement between the county of Niagara and the Kaihatsu Field Hockey Clinic, the County Attorney will review said agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Kaihatsu Field Hockey Clinic, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the county of Niagara and the Kaihatsu Field Hockey Clinic, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it fu11her\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the license agreement between the County of Niagara and the Kaihatsu Field Hockey Clinic.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-079-17":"Resolution No. IF-079-17\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nAUTHORIZATION TO PROCEED WITH\nTHE NIAGARA COUNTY WEST CANAL MARINA & PARKS PADDLE SPORT\nLAUNCH PROJECT- PHASE II POWER AND WATER INSTALLATION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Power Coalition (\"NPC\") and the New York Power Authority (\"NYPA\") agreed to a licensing settlement for the next 50 years for the Niagara Power Project, and\nWHEREAS, that settlement provides Niagara County to receive $390,000 annually to fund economic development projects along the Greenway Trail, and\nWHEREAS, the Erie Canal waterfront is a shared natural, scenic and recreational asset for Niagara County and the town of Pendleton and great strides have been taken by both communities to work collaboratively to study, plan and implement development and preservation effmis that will enhance accessibility while maintaining the natural integrity of the area, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Parks Depruiment is proposing to improve waterfront access and recreational oppo11unities offered to the community at the West Canal Marina & Park site; this proposal is the next step in utilizing greenway funds to execute the goals of the Niagara County Comprehensive Plan and the comprehensive plan in the \"Vision for the Amherst/Pendleton Waterfront Revitalization Area,\" and WH~REAS, Niagara County Parks has a series of parks throughout the County, serving the families of Niagara County and Western New York; West Canal Marina & Park is a 30 acre park that serves as a marina and parkland, and is open to all residents; it is an ideal setting for picnicking, kayaking, boating, canoeing and more, and\nWHEREAS, the current proposal is a \"Phase II\" to the original Park Paddle Spo11 Launch Project; the second phase includes enhancement to the boat launch area with the installation of power and water, and\nWHEREAS, the West Canal Marina & Park Paddle Sp011 Launch - Phase II Power and Water Installation Project meets the guiding principles of the Niagara River Greenway Commission, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara River Greenway Commission's guiding principles promote high-quality, ecologically sensitive and sustainable activities and development, and\nWHEREAS, the proposed West Canal Marina & Park Paddle Sport Launch - Phase II Power and Water Installation Project will increase traffic to the County's waterfront and parks; restoring environmental integrity, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County's Park request has the suppo11 of the ~own of Pendleton, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby authorize the Niagara County Buildings and Grounds Department to proceed with the request for sponsorship through the Niagara County's Ad Hoc Committee on Recreation and Tourism and the Niagara Power Coalition's Host Community Standing Committee for funding in an amount not to exceed $100,000.00 for the Niagara County West Canal Marina & Parks Paddle Sp011 Launch - Phase II Power and Water Installation Project when presented to the Host Community Standing Committee, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vi11uoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IF-080-17":"Resolution No. IF-080-17\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees\nDated: May 2, 2017\nTO CONVEY COUNTY PROPERTY LOCATED AT\n50 MAIN STREET, LOCKPORT, NEW YORK TO THE CITY OF LOCKPORT\nWHEREAS, the parcel of prope1ty located at 50 Main Street, Lockport, NY was pa11 of the land package to purchase 20-40 East A venue and 111 Main Street, Lockport, New York, and\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara has no plan to develop or utilize this property, and\nWHEREAS, the County by resolution IF-116-15 declared 50 Main Street, Lockport, New York, surplus prope11y, and\nWHEREAS, the Lockpmt Industrial Development Corp. passed a resolution on March 23, 2017 authorizing the mayor of the city of Lockport, New York to convey the prope1ty located at 50 Main Street, Lockport, New York from the County for the municipal use and utilization of the property, and\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara hereby determines the transfer of said parcel to the city of Lockport would be in the best interest of the County, and\nWHEREAS, the county of Niagara finds and determines prior to the conveyance as protection of the County's interests the execution and delivery of a transfer and indemnity agreement received from the city of Lockpmt is necessary, and\nWHEREAS, the terms and conditions under which the property is to be conveyed to the city of Lockpo11 are subject to the final review and approval by the Niagara County Attorney's Office and the attorney of the city of Lockport, and must include legally binding assurances of the city of Lockport holding haimless and fully indemnifying the county of Niagara from any liability, damage, claims or judgments arising after the transfer of said prope1ty from the condition and use of the Prope1ty including the condition and use of the Property thereafter by the city of Lockport, its transferees, grantees, successors and assigns, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the county of Niagara agrees to sell the premises located at 50 Main Street, Lockport, parcel SBL# 109.13-3-5, in city of Lockport to the city of Lockport for the amount of one dollar ($1.00), and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the county of Niagai\u00b7a agrees to transfer title to the premises in \"as is condition,\" and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that the city of Lockp01t agrees to maintain the facilities and grounds, pay all utilities, and assume all liability issues for the facilities and grounds, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the city of Lockpmt shall use such premises for the utilization by the city of Lockport for municipal purposes, and be it futther\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to convey said property to the city of Lockport, by the execution and delivery of a deed and all other required documents for transfer of 50 Main Street, Lockport, New York, SBL#109.13-3-5.\nMoved by Collins, seconded by Nemi.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-019-17":"Resolution No. IL-019-17\nFrom: Legislator Anthony J. Nemi and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF 2017 COMMUNITY FUNDING FOR PENDLETON\nORGANIZATIONS THROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDS\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New York statute, and\nWHEREAS, the town of Pendleton is blessed with various not-for-profit organizations that promote civil engagement and community pride, and\nWHEREAS, these organizations play an impo1tant role throughout the community by teaching and promoting good citizenship, honoring veterans, helping seniors, preserving history, promoting local business, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Lions Club would like to pmchase and donate child handicap playground equipment for Pendleton Town Park, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Food Pantry would like to pmchase additional food stock for their yearly family in need distribution, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Business & Community Association would sponsor and promote its annual Community Faire & Business Expo, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Veterans, would like to build a pe1manent monument to honor all veterans, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Historical Society would like to improve their exhibit area for visitors and school children, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Seniors and Wendelville Seniors would like improve and expand their monthly programs to include wellness and fitness instructions, and\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Farm & Home Days committee is planning a two day celebration promoting the\ngood life and community spirit as well as celebrating the 1901  1! year of the Town founding, and\nWHEREAS, Pets Alive WNY rescues' cats and kittens and provides housing, food, medicine, and affection while finding adopt homes, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is interested in supporting efforts of community service, promoting local business, and create a sense of pride throughout the community, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the following Pendleton organizations as follows:  Pendleton\nFood Pantry  $500.00\nPendleton Community & Business Assoc.  $1,500.00\nPendleton Lions Club  $500.00\nPendleton Veterans  $500.00\nPendleton Historical Society  $1,000.00\nPendleton Seniors  $500.00\nWendelville Seniors  $500.00\nPendleton Farm & Home Days  $1,000.00\nPets Alive WNY  $500.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 201 7 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATIONS FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance- Committed Funds  $6,500.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $6,500.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-020-17":"Resolution No. IL-020-17\nFrom:  Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Kathryn L. Lance and Economic Development\nCommittee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE CHAMBER OF COMMERCE OF THE\nTONA WANDA'S THROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Chamber of Commerce of the Tonawanda's provides a strong source of services, support and networking for business and industry in the Tonawanda's, and\nWHEREAS, the Chamber of Commerce of the Tonawanda's are seeking assistance to help purchase and install a Logitech USB camera/audio system which will allow for a complete video conferencing service in their conference room, and\nWHEREAS, having this equipment will benefit all members and the board of directors and help to increase profits, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Chamber of Commerce of the Tonawanda's as follows:\nLogitech USB Camera/ Audio System  $3,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 201 7 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $3,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $3,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-024-17":"Resolution No. IL-024-17\nFrom:  Legislators Anthony J. Nemi, Wm. Keith McNall, William J. Collin and Economic Development\nCommittee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF FUNDING LOCKPORT IN BLOOM\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New York statute, and\nWHEREAS, Lockp01t in Bloom is a not-for-profit organization, chattered in 2005, that promotes the beautification of Lockport by encouraging residents to share with others their beautiful and creative gardens, and\nWHEREAS, by encouraging neighborhood beautification, Lockport in Bloom helps promote community pride by bringing people to the area to view the many beautiful gardens, and\nWHEREAS, through their annual Garden Festival, Lockp01t in Bloom brings thousands of visitors to the City of Lockp01t to view the 40 plus gardens that are put on display every summer, and\nWHEREAS, local businesses and restaurants benefit greatly from the increase in foot traffic that Lockpo1t in Bloom generates throughout the City of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is interested in supp01ting efforts that generate increased revenue for businesses and create a sense of pride throughout the community, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Lockport in Bloom as follows:\nLockport in Bloom  $2,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $2,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $2,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-025-17":"Resolution No. IL-025-17\nFrom: Legislator Randy R. Bradt and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF FUNDING THE LAKE ONTARIO PRO-AM SERIES\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New York State Finance Law\u00a7 99-h, and\nWHEREAS, this Legislature is committed to utilizing said funds for economic and community development goals that enhance our community and make it more desirable to businesses looking to expand and relocate operations, and\nWHEREAS, the economies of New York State and Niagara County are heavily reliant upon Lake Ontario, Lake Erie, the Niagara River waterway, and the Tonawanda Creek, and\nWHEREAS, the economic impact of sport fishing on those counties bordering Lake Ontario, namely the Counties of Niagara, Orleans, Monroe, Wayne, Cayuga, Oswego, and Jefferson, was $54 million in 2007, per research by Cornell University's Department of Natural Resources, and\nWHEREAS, these sport fishing events benefit hotels, restaurants and retail businesses throughout Niagara County and provide substantial sales tax revenue, and\nWHEREAS, the Lake Ontario Pro-Am Series is among the most prominent of these fishing derbies, and specifically features the Lake Ontario Pro-Am Salmon Team Tournament in Niagara County May 19-21 of this year, and\nWHEREAS, the 2017 Lake Ontario Pro-Am Series includes a \"Reelin' for a Cure\" fishing derby in Olcott that will raise funds for cancer research, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County disburses \u00a7 99-h funding for the purpose of defraying costs associated with development and promotion of the 2017 Lake Ontario Pro-Am Series:\nNTCC- Sport Fishing  $7,500.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $7,500.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $7,500.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent-Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-027-17":"Resolution No. IL-027-17\nFrom: Legislator Jason A. Zona and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF NIAGARA BEAUTIFICATION COMMITTEE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Town of Niagara Beautification Committee works to enhance the beauty of the Business District in the Town of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, each year, tens of thousands of visitors, as well as Niagara County residents, utilize the Military Road retail business corridor, and\nWHEREAS, these shoppers generate revenue for local businesses, as well as large amounts of sales tax receipts for the county, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Niagara Beautification Committee strives to make a pleasant, welcoming experience for all residents and visitors alike to the Town of Niagara and Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, this year the Town of Niagara Beautification Committee is planning to enhance the area with forty-three (43) potted planters placed throughout the business corridor of the town, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature wishes to suppmt the Town of Niagara Beautification Committee's mission to beautify and enhance the business district in Niagara County through the use of casino revenues, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget:\nTown of Niagara Beautification Committee  $6,450.00\nand be it further\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $6,450.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $6,450.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-029-17":"Resolution No. IL-029-17\nFrom: Legislator Michael A. Hill and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF ROYALTON THROUGH\nTHE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the town of Royalton is requesting funding for updates to the Veterans Park in the Hamlet of Gasport, and\nWHEREAS, the Veterans Park would like to add new playground equipment and woodchips to the playgrounds and upgrades to the horseshoe pits, and\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the town of Royalton as follows:\nTown of Royalton  $5,000.00\nand be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 201 7 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $5,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $5,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-030-17":"Resolution No. IL-030-17\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA RIVER REGION CHAMBER OF COMMERCE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce has recently expanded their advertising line to the Lewiston, Youngstown, Sanborn and Ransomville region to showcase the great natural beauty and the local businesses and organizational sponsoring exciting events that draw thousands of people from all over Western New York and Southern Ontario, and\nWHEREAS, an opportunity has risen for the Chamber to place a featured listing on the Niagara Tourism Convention Corporation website geared toward visitors of the region and help generate a positive economic impact which includes first class restaurants and interesting shops, and\nWHEREAS, the communities within the boundaries of the Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce draw thousands of visitors every year, bringing tourism dollars to the area which are vital to the Niagara County economy, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature supports funding to the Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce for marketing purposes as follows:\nNiagara River Region Chamber of Commerce  $2,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 201 7 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01 Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $2,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15 Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $2,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-031-17":"Resolution No. IL-031-17\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE IDSTORIC LEWISTON JAZZ FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the village of Lewiston is blessed with great natural beauty and its local organizations sponsor a multitude of extremely exciting events which draw thousands of people from all over Western New York, Southern Ontario and beyond, and\nWHEREAS, these festival events generate a positive economic impact on the businesses which include first class restaurants and interesting shops, and\nWHEREAS, the Historic Lewiston Jazz Festival is the premier Jazz Festival in Western New York and has the total supp01i of the residents and companies for funding this regional music jewel, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature casino fund contribution will support the marketing effo1i of the Lewiston Jazz Festival Board of Directors, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature support this outstanding musical event as follows:\nHistoric Lewiston Jazz Festival  $2,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01 Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $2,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15 Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $2,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-032-17":"Resolution No. IL-032-17\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON ART FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the village of Lewiston is blessed with great natural beauty and its local organizations sponsor a multitude of extremely exciting events which draw thousands of people from all over Western New York, Southern Ontario and beyond, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Art Festival, which is sponsored by the Lewiston Council on the Arts, has become a highlight of the summer season in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Art Festival attracts artists and vendors from across the country, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Art Festival is an economic driver that suppo1ts jobs, generates government revenue and is a cornerstone of local tourism, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature casino fund contribution will support the continuous marketing effort of the Lewiston Alt Festival by the Lewiston Council on the Arts, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County suppo1ts this outstanding Alt event as follows:\nLewiston Art Festival Marketing  $2,000.00\nand be it fwther\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $2,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:  ,\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $2,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-033-17":"Resolution No. IL-033-17\nFrom: Legislator Clyde L. Burmaster and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE YOUNGSTOWN LIONS CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDS\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club has made available a Community park area in Youngstown for many years, it now finds itself in need of major restoration which will take place over many years, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club has studied the various needs and has planned a joint project with the village of Youngstown for the next few years to restore the park to safe and fun use, and\nWHEREAS, this is a significant unde11aking which will enhance the quality of life for all, not just Village residents, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club has reached out for help with this project and in consideration of all the good Lions do every day, it is only right for the Legislature to assist when possible, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Casino development funds be granted to the Youngstown Lions Club in the amount of $1,500 to assist in the restoration of the Youngstown Lions Park, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget:\nYoungstown Lions Club  $1,500.00\nand be it fu.tt her\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $1,500.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $1,500.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-034-17":"Resolution No. IL-034-17\nFrom: Legislator Clyde L. Burmaster and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF YOUNGSTOWN VILLAGE CONCERT SERIES FOR THE\nSUMMER OF 2017\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, there has been in existence for many years a full schedule of weekly musical concerts in the park in Youngstown for many years which have been very well attended by people from all over Niagara County as by visitors to Old Fort Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, the entertainment provided is of 1st class and makes for a enjoyable summer evening outdoor entertainment for all, and\nWHEREAS, funding for these events is always a challenge and many dedicated volunteers diligently have worked very hard to ensure these concerts continue, and\nWHEREAS, it is the wish of the Legislature to always be suppmtive of our communities as best we can, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $1,000.00 be granted to the Youngstown Village Concert Series from Casino Funds for costs incurred in continuing this most worthy activity, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget:\nYoungstown Village Concert Series  $1,000.00\nand be it further\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $1,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $1,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-035-17":"Resolution No. IL-035-17\nFrom: Legislator Clyde L. Burmaster and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF PURCHASING OF A ELECTRONIC SIGN FOR THE\nRANSOMVILLE LIBRARY TO ANNOUNCE COMMUNITY ACTIVITY AND ALERTS\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, it is very important to every community to have a central information point and increasingly it has become a major source of providing emergency alerts and activity schedules by use of Electronic Signs for these announcements, and\nWHEREAS, the old fashioned manually lettered sign was destroyed by severe weather, which will require a new sign, and\nWHEREAS, it has been decided to replace the sign with modern electronic sign which can be accessed indoors to program the messages, and\nWHEREAS, signs of this kind can cost as much as $10,000 and higher, it is decided that the technology provided by a sign in the $15,000 range will satisfy the immediate and near future, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that available Casino funds be used in the amount of $7,000.00 to be added to other community funds to purchase this sign, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget:\nRansomville Library  Electronic Sign  $7,000.00\nand be it further\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $7,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $7,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-036-17":"Resolution No. IL-036-17\nFrom: Legislator Clyde L. Bmmaster and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF FUNDING TO THE OLD FORT NIAGARA ASSOCIATION FOR\nCONSTRUCTION OF A BUILDING TO HOUSE NEEDED SAFETY EQUIPMENT\nWHEREAS, Youngstown N.Y. is one of the most historic places in these United States, and\nWHEREAS, Youngstown is the location of Old Fort Niagara, the oldest continuously occupied military fort this side of the Mississippi which draws over 115,000 visitors per year to see and leam about history of the United States, and\nWHEREAS, it is the responsibility of the Association to provide for the safety of the visitors as well as to protect the structures contained therein, and\nWHEREAS, there is an immediate need for a strncture that would house much required fire fighting apparatus but limited funds are available to complete the project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in the amount of $7,000.00 through the use of Casino funds to finish the job,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the sum of $7,000.00 be granted to the Old Fort Niagara Association from the stated monies to complete the construction of a building, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Develo~ment 2017 budget:\nOld Fmt Niagara Association  $7,000.00\nand be it further\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $7,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $7,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-037-17":"Resolution No. IL-037-17\nFrom: Legislator Clyde L. Butmaster and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE RANSOMVILLE LIBRARY HISTORY PRESERVATION\nPROGRAM AND LIBRARY IMPROVEMENTS THROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDS\nWHEREAS, Libraries remain the center of any community for reading, media and history, and\nWHEREAS, Libraries are more than just books but are used for meetings, celebrations, small community events, etc, and\nWHEREAS, some Libraries also become safe havens for historical artifacts and documents so important to preserving our heritage, and\nWHEREAS, the Ransomville Library has a very active Library as well as a very active history preservation, requiring ongoing material costs for special programs, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available to help offset these costs through the Casino funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a grant of $1,000.00 to each, Library and History Program for a total of $2,000.00 be awarded from the Casino funds for Program expenses in the year 2017, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget:\nRansomville Library  $2,000.00\nand be it further\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $2,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $2,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent -Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-038-17":"Resolution No. IL-038-17\nFrom: Legislator Clyde L. Bwmaster and Economic Development Committee\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF TllE USE OF CASINO FUNDS TO PURCHASE LIBRARY\nMATERIALS FOR THE YOUNGSTOWN FREE LIBRARY FOR 2017\nWHEREAS, the Youngstown Free Library has served the village of Youngstown for many years, providing a valuable resource center for its residents, and\nWHEREAS, the Library is the center of the Village for those reading books as well as many using the media resources as well as computers to satisfy recreational and educational needs, and\nWHEREAS, each year it becomes increasingly more difficult to provide research materials with limited funding, and\nWHEREAS, if the library is able to continue to be the resource to the community it must actively seek finances from residents and with support from the friends of the Library will be able to keep abreast with new technology, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that funds are available through the development funds to award $1,000.00 to the Youngstown Library for the year 2017 for expenses of reference materials, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2017 budget:\nYoungstown Free Library  $1,000.00\nand be it further\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $1,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $1,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Zona.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse & Wydysh","IL-050-17":"Resolution No. IL-050-17\nFrom: Legislators John Syracuse, Clyde L. Bmmaster, David E. Godfrey, Wm. Keith McNall, Randy R. Bradt, et al.\nDated: May 2, 2017\nRESOLUTION URGING PRESIDENT TRUMP TO IMMEDIATELY APPOINT\nNEW COMMISSIONERS TO THE INTERNATIONAL JOINT COMMISSION\nAND SAID COMMISSIONERS TO ENACT POLICIES RESCINDING\nTHE IJC'S 'PLAN 2014' LAKE LEVEL MANAGEMENT SCHEME\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has, on multiple occasions, expressed its opposition to the International Joint Commission's imposition of Plan 2014, most notably through the passage of Resolution IL-061-15, \"Expressing the Opposition of the Niagara County Legislature to the IJC Plan 2014 & Supporting the Efforts of U.S. Representative Chris Collins to Block Enactment of the Sarne,\" IL-041-13, \"Request to the International Joint Commission to Reject the Proposed Plan 2014 in its Entirety,\" and IL-035-14, \"Niagara-Orleans Regional Alliance (NORA) Requests Governor Andrew M. Cuomo to Veto the International Joint Commission's Plan 2014 for Controlling the Levels of Lake Ontario Should the US Department of State Recommend It ,\" and\nWHEREAS, said resolutions clearly warned \"qualified observers with expertise in civil engineering have indicated Plan 2014 will have a devastating impact on the communities along the Lake's southern shoreline\" and \"Plan 2014 will increase the frequency of raising and lowering the water levels in Lake Ontario and the fluctuations in water levels will result in increased erosion damage to the shoreline, including the prope1ties of businesses and homeowners,\" and\nWHEREAS, said resolutions noted \"the effects of Plan 2014 span six counties in Upstate New York and would cause damage to land assessed at nearly $4 billion\", and\nWHEREAS, said resolutions also warned \"the IJC working group stated the proposed lake levels are going to reduce recreational boating/sport fishing benefits by 25%, resulting in a devastating negative impact on those economics in Niagara County which is estimated at $30 million per year, and in Orleans County which is estimated $12 million per year, thus an area-wide loss ofrevenue of over $10.5 million annually,\" and\nWHEREAS, in 2013 this Legislature did warn that the goal of Plan 2014 was to enrich St. Lawrence River hydropower producers at the much greater expense of South Shore property owners, noting, \"[Plan 2014] greatly decreases protections that exist in the current plan, replacing them with a plan that results in the transfer of prosperity from Niagara County along with other Lake Ontario communities to Hydropower (NYP A and others) who will realize a benefit of approximately $5.3 million per year on average,\" and\nWHEREAS, due to drastically higher water levels in Lake Ontario, the counties of Niagara and Orleans did, on April 20 of this year, the first year of implementation of Plan 2014, declare States of Emergency due to a high probability of flooding in lakeshore communities in addition to large-scale lakeshore erosion impacting the integrity of banks and presenting hazards to both individuals on land and boaters, and\nWHEREAS, various lakeshore communities did, on April 12 and 13, issue 500-foot \"no wake zone\" orders, forbidding boat traffic closer than 500 feet from shore except for the purpose of docking, and\nWHEREAS, the Corps of Engineers has refused to release Lake Ontario water into the St. Lawrence River at an accelerated pace, which could relieve flooding concerns, due to rules set in Plan 2014, and\nWHEREAS, the implementation of Plan 2014 has proven an absolute disaster for our communities, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara does hereby call upon the remammg U.S. Commissioners and all three Canadian Commissioners of the IJC to resign, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara does hereby call upon President Trump to demand said resignations or fire the U.S. Commissioners immediately, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara does hereby call upon the United States Congress to conduct investigative hearings into the IJC's culpability in a manmade disaster on a financial scale comparable with the contamination of the Flint, Michigan, water supply, and that has placed lives in immediate danger, and\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara does hereby call upon President Trump to replace said failed U.S. Commissioners with representatives who will appropriately weigh protecting prope1ty owners' rights and prope1ty values with environmental considerations, and with such experience in civil engineering and other appropriate fields as to be qualified to make determinations about lake levels, and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county of Niagara does hereby call upon the International Joint Commission to immediately suspend Plan 2014 and reimplement previous regulatory rules, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the county ofNiagara does hereby call upon the Corps of Engineers to take immediate steps to release water from Lake Ontario, and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the county of Niagara shall forward copies of this Resolution to President Trump; Secretary of State Rex Tillerson; U.S. Rep. Chris Collins; U.S. Senator Charles E. Schumer; U.S. Senator Kirsten E. Gillibrand; International Joint Commission Chair Lana Pollock; Commissioner Rich Moy; Commission Chair Gordon Walker; Commissioner Benoit Bouchard; Commissioner Richard A. Morgan; and all others deemed necessary and proper.\nMoved by Burmaster, seconded by Godfrey, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to refen\"ing it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Burmaster, seconded by Godfrey.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Syracuse and Wydysh"};
